# Fix Transcoder: Server Closes Connection and Decoding Failures

## Problem 1: Missing `tcUrl` in Connect Command (Server Closes Connection)

The log shows:
```
[INFO ] Output connected: 360p
[DEBUG] Connection closed by peer
[WARN ] Output disconnected: 360p
```

Looking at the code in `setup_pusher_callbacks()`:
```cpp
connect_obj["app"] = amf0::Value::String("live");
connect_obj["type"] = amf0::Value::String("nonprivate");
// MISSING: connect_obj["tcUrl"] = ...
```

The connect command is missing the required `tcUrl` field. Most RTMP servers (including the target server `live.vaughnsoft.net`) require `tcUrl` to be present in the connect command object, and will reject/close connections that don't include it.

Compare to the working relay code in `relay_manager.cpp` which includes `tcUrl`:
```cpp
connect_obj["app"] = amf0::Value::String(app);
connect_obj["type"] = amf0::Value::String("nonprivate");
connect_obj["tcUrl"] = amf0::Value::String(url.substr(0, path_start + app.size() + 1));
```

### Fix for Problem 1

In `src/transcode/transcoder.cpp`, modify `setup_pusher_callbacks()` to include `tcUrl`:

```cpp
void Transcoder::setup_pusher_callbacks(Output* output) {
    // Store the URL for use in callback
    std::string url = output->config.rtmp_url;

    output->pusher->set_connected_callback([this, out = output, url]() {
        Logger::info("Output connected: ", out->config.name);
        out->connected = true;

        // Parse URL to extract app and build tcUrl
        size_t proto_end = url.find("://");
        size_t host_start = proto_end + 3;
        size_t path_start = url.find('/', host_start);
        std::string path = path_start != std::string::npos ? url.substr(path_start) : "/";

        size_t app_end = path.find('/', 1);
        std::string app = app_end != std::string::npos ? path.substr(1, app_end - 1) : path.substr(1);

        // Extract stream name from URL
        std::string stream_name = app_end != std::string::npos ? path.substr(app_end + 1) : "stream";

        // Build tcUrl (protocol://host:port/app)
        std::string tcUrl = url.substr(0, path_start) + "/" + app;

        // Send connect command with tcUrl
        rtmp::CommandMessage connect_cmd;
        connect_cmd.name = "connect";
        connect_cmd.transaction_id = 1;

        amf0::Value::ObjectType connect_obj;
        connect_obj["app"] = amf0::Value::String(app);
        connect_obj["type"] = amf0::Value::String("nonprivate");
        connect_obj["tcUrl"] = amf0::Value::String(tcUrl);  // THIS IS THE FIX
        connect_cmd.arguments.push_back(amf0::Value::Object(connect_obj));

        out->pusher->send_command(connect_cmd);
        out->pusher->flush();

        // Store stream name for publish command
        out->stream_name = stream_name;
    });
    // ... rest of callbacks
}
```

Also need to use the correct stream name when sending the publish command:
```cpp
// In the message callback, when sending publish:
publish_cmd.arguments.push_back(amf0::Value::String(out->stream_name));  // Use extracted stream name
publish_cmd.arguments.push_back(amf0::Value::String("live"));
```

## Problem 2: AAC Decode Failures (Error 4098)

```
[DEBUG] AAC decode frame failed: 4098
```

Error 4098 = `AAC_DEC_UNSUPPORTED_FORMAT` from FDK-AAC. The issue is that the decoder is receiving raw AAC frames but may not be properly configured, or the input data format is wrong.

Looking at `on_audio_data()`:
```cpp
if (aac_packet_type == 0) {
    // Audio Specific Config
    const uint8_t* asc_data = data + 2;
    size_t asc_size = size - 2;

    if (!audio_decoder_->initialize(asc_data, asc_size)) {
        Logger::error("Failed to initialize audio decoder with ASC");
    }
}
```

The AudioSpecificConfig (ASC) parsing looks correct, but when decoding raw AAC data, the decoder expects raw AAC frames without ADTS headers, while the AAC data from FLV is raw AAC-LC frames.

The issue might be:
1. The decoder is not properly initialized before receiving raw frames
2. The raw AAC data needs to be wrapped in ADTS for FDK-AAC

